A generator body's locals become cells: the cell's type comes from `typeof` of its first write

The body of a generator is a state machine of arms, and a local declared in one arm is not
there in the arm that a later pull runs. So a local whose value has to live across a `yield`
is a cell of the generator's own body. What the cell's type is had been the open question:
the first attempt took it from the type of the value the first write holds in `step`, which
asks for the step function to be built before the body that creates the stream, and that
order is not the one the machine is built in.

The type now comes from `typeof` of the first write's expression, written in the cell's own
type position. That is the same rule a written type follows everywhere: a type position is
resolved in one place (`builder_generics.cpp#Builder::resolveTypeNode`, reached from seven
call sites -- the `typeof` inference landed earlier is what makes it a type position that is
inferred without being evaluated). The creation body makes the cell with `:var/new/empty`
(operator `oper::VarNewEmpty`, `include/camel/core/operator_table.h`) and evaluates nothing
(design principles 5.4); the value is written where the declaration stands, in the arm that
reaches it, so a pull reads the value the pull before it wrote.

What the change touches:

- `gct/builder.cpp#Builder::visitGeneratorBody`: `addCell` for a declaration of the body is
  now `addTypedCell` -- `NREF(name, TYPE(typeof <first write>))` -- and the hoisting no
  longer needs the declaration's value to be a constant: the type no longer depends on the
  value being evaluable where the stream is created. A declaration the body reads across a
  `yield` is no longer refused; its value is written where the statement stands.
- `gir/builder_expr.cpp#Builder::visitNRefNode`: an `NREF` whose first child is a `TYPE` is a
  cell of that type (`Builder::newVariableOfType`), not a name to look up and not an array.
- `gir/builder.h`, `builtin/modules/globals.cpp`, `builtin/operators/ops.*`,
  `builtin/executors/builtin.cpp`: `:var/new/empty` -- a `var T` whose slot holds no value
  yet, `{1, {false}}, {0, {}}` with the signature `<T>() => var T`.
- `gir/builder_generics.cpp#Builder::inferTypeOf`: the comment now names the three things
  that make the scratch graph safe to drop (its diagnostics go to a sink of its own; graph,
  chain and the four scopes are restored as a whole; the entries keyed by its address are
  erased and it is no body of the unit).
- `gir/builder_diagnostics.cpp`: a cell is not a `T!` that nothing takes. A `var T!` keeps
  the error it holds, so the statement that makes or fills a cell is not an unused fallible
  value -- otherwise every generator local of a fallible type warns on the cell that holds it.

The invariant the arms rest on is checked where the arms are made: a cell is written before
it is read. Source order does not say it -- the cell's name is bound where the stream is
created, so a statement can name one whose declaration stands after it -- so `fill` checks
each statement against the cells whose declaration stands at or after it. That program is not
expressible in the language (a name stands only after its declaration), so the check is an
internal error, not a diagnostic, as the ruling of 2026-10-30 asks; a diagnostic is owed the
day a declaration without a value is allowed. Without the check the read found the cell made
and empty and the program printed a zero out of an uninitialised slot.

What is still refused, and why, in this one place:

- a loop state whose first value is computed (`loop j = i + 1` inside another loop): the cell
  and its type are in place, but the first value reads a cell of the enclosing loop across an
  arm boundary, and that read is not lowered yet -- measured: it does not fail to compile, it
  crashes the compiler at run time, so `FeatureNotSupported('generators')` still refuses it;
- a `for` in a generator body (the binder needs a cell of its own), a `while` with more than
  one state, a `yield` in a statement shape other than `if`/`match`/`loop`, and a name bound
  by a `match` arm;
- a declaration that binds more than one name, or that has no value, is still an arm local and
  reading it across a `yield` is still refused (`refuseLocalAcrossYield`).

Evidence: `test/cases/compile/stream/generators_local_across_yield.cml` is now a behaviour
case -- a local computed from a parameter, written before the first `yield` and read after it,
hands out `1` and then `6` -- where it used to be the case that pinned the refusal. Reversing
the typed cell (back to a cell made with the initializer's value and the old const-only
hoisting) makes exactly that case red again; the other 105 tests of the stream plan stay
green. The graph of a generator shows the cell as `OPER | var int` with no node of the
initializer in the body that creates the stream, which is what `stream.generator_cell_type_
from_first_write` holds. Before this change the same program was refused by
`FeatureNotSupported('generators')` at the declaration -- that refusal is what the case used
to pin, and `git stash` of the generator change turns the rewritten case red again with that
same message.

Open, and for the author: with the cell's name bound where the stream is created, a program
CAN name a cell before the statement that writes it (`yield e` written above `let e = 5`).
The ruling of 2026-10-30 says such a program cannot be written and so needs no diagnostic;
this commit holds that invariant with an internal error at the arm-construction site. If a
diagnostic is wanted instead, it is a small change at one place (`gct/builder.cpp`, `fill`).
