What is still refused, and the rule that shape broke: a loop state whose first value is
computed (`loop j = i + 1` inside another loop) is refused in the compiler today, and it is
refused because the shape that was tried instead was unacceptable: with the first value read
across an arm boundary and no lowering for that read, the program did not fail to compile --
it crashed the compiler at run time. The rule the lowering holds, and should hold: **a shape
whose cross-arm read is not lowered must be refused in the compiler, never left to the run
time.** So `!hoistable(init)` stays on the loop-state path (literal/lambda/`const as` only)
until that read is lowered; the type inference alone does not make the shape safe.

`fallibleTypeOf` in `reportUnusedFallibleValues` (a cell is not a `T!` that nothing takes) is
the classic "a new feature ships and immediately manufactures a false warning": making a local
a cell of a fallible type put two `UnusedFallibleValue` warnings on a program that used its
error, one for the cell the creation body makes and one for the write that fills it. Nothing
about the warning rule was wrong -- the rule never had to look at a `var T!` statement before.
If a later change sees this warning on a cell, it is this rule, not a regression.

The A/B that located it is worth copying: reverting **one file** of the half-finished feature
(`git checkout HEAD~1 -- src/compile/gct/builder.cpp`) rather than the whole commit leaves the
rest of the mechanism in place, so exactly one test goes red and the reason is unambiguous --
`stream.generator_local_across_yield` refused by `FeatureNotSupported`, 105 green -- and
restoring it gives 106/106. Reverting half a feature by file locates it better than reverting
all of it.

The refusal sites did not move: `FeatureNotSupported` is still emitted from three places in
`gct/builder.cpp` and `refuseLocalAcrossYield` is still called once. What changed is the set of
declarations that reaches it -- a single-name declaration with a value is a cell now, so it
never becomes an arm local; a declaration that binds several names or has no value still does.
